    deepExplanation: "React reconciliation is the process of comparing the previously rendered element tree with the next element tree and determining which component/host instances can be reused, updated, inserted, moved, or removed. React primarily relies on element type and key to establish identity, while Fiber provides the internal representation used to perform this work incrementally.\n\n```tsx\ntype User = { id: string; name: string };\n\nfunction UserList({ users }: { users: User[] }) {\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {users.map(user => (\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}\n```\nWhen `users` changes, React reconciles the new children against the previous children. Stable `id` keys let React preserve the corresponding list item identity instead of treating every row as a new item.\n\nSenior interview focus: explain the observable React behavior first, then the relevant reconciliation mechanism and its production trade-offs.",
