    deepExplanation: "React Strict Mode is a development-time tool that intentionally exercises certain rendering and effect behavior to expose impure render logic, missing cleanup, and other bugs. Its exact checks depend on the React version and development environment, so it should not be described as a production rendering mode or as a performance feature.\n\n```tsx\nfunction Timer() {\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const id = window.setInterval(() => {\n      console.log(\"tick\");\n    }, 1000);\n\n    return () => window.clearInterval(id);\n  }, []);\n\n  return <span>Running</span>;\n}\n```\nDevelopment checks can expose missing cleanup by causing setup/cleanup behavior to be exercised more aggressively. The correct fix is to make the effect lifecycle safe, not to add flags that hide Strict Mode.\n\nSenior interview focus: explain the observable React behavior first, then the relevant reconciliation mechanism and its production trade-offs.",
